1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:11,033
Seventeen episodes. From win rate to walk-forward, from drawdown to the kill switch.

2
00:00:11,484 --> 00:00:18,485
And the most honest part: our own example system lost money across 42 markets.

3
00:00:18,935 --> 00:00:25,543
Today we see why that loss is the biggest lesson. The last episode of season one.

4
00:00:26,543 --> 00:00:31,533
Hello, this is episode eighteen of the SPIDER Quant Trading series.

5
00:00:31,982 --> 00:00:36,894
Last time: live versus backtest, and the kill switch.

6
00:00:37,344 --> 00:00:40,349
Today, the whole season's journey in one place.

7
00:00:41,349 --> 00:00:47,879
Part one, the foundation. Quant means rules, numbers, and tests.

8
00:00:48,329 --> 00:00:55,487
Win rate alone is incomplete; look at it with payoff. Drawdown tells you how deep the pain goes.

9
00:00:55,937 --> 00:01:02,938
And position sizing: stop first, then size. A small, fixed risk per trade.

10
00:01:03,938 --> 00:01:09,893
Part two, testing. The rules of backtesting, and risk of ruin.

11
00:01:10,342 --> 00:01:16,378
Monte Carlo showed that the same trades in a different order can give very different drawdowns.

12
00:01:16,827 --> 00:01:23,985
The report card taught us not to judge a system by one number. And overfitting, the biggest trap.

13
00:01:24,985 --> 00:01:30,314
Part three, our own system. First in Excel, then in Python.

14
00:01:30,764 --> 00:01:36,720
Then out-of-sample and walk-forward, so the system does not just memorize old data.

15
00:01:37,169 --> 00:01:42,812
And market regime: the same system behaves differently in trend and range.

16
00:01:43,812 --> 00:01:50,892
Part four, portfolio. Spreading risk across many markets reduced the swings.

17
00:01:51,342 --> 00:01:56,827
Size by volatility, so no single market eats the whole risk.

18
00:01:57,277 --> 00:02:03,964
Costs showed that even a small expense can swallow an edge. And the kill switch: when to stop.

19
00:02:04,964 --> 00:02:12,122
Now the biggest point. Our breakout system ended at minus 91.9R across 42 markets.

20
00:02:12,572 --> 00:02:17,561
Only 17 markets were profitable. This is not a system worth selling to anyone.

21
00:02:18,012 --> 00:02:23,888
But that is exactly the job of quant: to learn the truth from data before putting money in.

22
00:02:24,888 --> 00:02:30,845
The most common mistake: see one good backtest and go live with all your money.

23
00:02:31,295 --> 00:02:39,106
The right order: idea, backtest, out-of-sample, small live, and then slowly increase size.

24
00:02:40,106 --> 00:02:45,748
What next? Take one simple idea of your own and put it through every step of this season.

25
00:02:46,198 --> 00:02:51,684
Write down every result, good or bad. No quant without a journal.

26
00:02:52,134 --> 00:02:59,762
Season two comes next month, with deeper topics: data-mining bias, automation, and more.

27
00:03:00,762 --> 00:03:03,844
Thank you for staying with us for eighteen episodes.

28
00:03:04,293 --> 00:03:09,936
If the series helped, subscribe and share the playlist with friends.

29
00:03:10,387 --> 00:03:15,793
Our Telegram and Instagram are in the description. See you in season two.

